# PRIORITY 8: PROGRESSIVE SECTION LOADING AND RESPONSIVE PERFORMANCE

The website currently loads too much content and motion at once.

Optimise it so sections and heavy assets load when they are relevant.

## 8.1 Use progressive loading, not click-only loading

My preferred loading strategy is:

- Keep the page structure, section headings and navigation anchors available.
- Load lightweight section shells first.
- Load heavier content when the section approaches the viewport.
- Load large image previews when they become relevant.
- Load expensive 3D scenes only when required.
- If a visitor clicks a navigation category, immediately prioritise and load that target section.
- Once a section has been loaded, retain it appropriately so navigation remains fast.

Do not make sections permanently inaccessible until a user clicks something.

Do not leave large blank areas or broken scroll positions while a section loads.

## 8.2 Lazy-load images globally

Inspect every image-bearing component across the web application, including:

- Landing page images.
- Smartphone images.
- Preview images.
- Branding images.
- User-uploaded images.
- Studio thumbnails.
- Library previews.
- Workspace images.
- Agent images.
- Generated media.
- Any other image assets.

Implement a consistent image-loading strategy.

Requirements:

- Lazy-load below-the-fold images.
- Eagerly load genuinely critical above-the-fold assets.
- Load only active and nearby carousel items first.
- Reserve image dimensions to prevent layout shift.
- Use responsive asset sizes.
- Optimise file formats and image quality.
- Add meaningful alt text for informative images.
- Use empty alt text for decorative images.
- Provide loading and failure fallbacks.
- Avoid unnecessary duplicate downloads.
- Do not turn important local assets into fragile remote dependencies.

The existing mobile showcase preloads all screen images on mount. Replace this eager preload with progressive, relevance-based loading.

## 8.3 Adaptive animation quality

Angel must adapt to different devices and graphics capabilities.

Create sensible performance tiers:

**High-performance devices**
- Richer 3D emblem rendering.
- Higher visual detail.
- Smooth transitions.
- Controlled particle and lighting effects.

**Mid-range devices**
- Reduced particle counts.
- Lower canvas resolution.
- Reduced motion complexity.
- Efficient CSS transforms.

**Low-powered devices or unsupported WebGL**
- Lightweight CSS 3D or 2D fallback.
- Fewer simultaneous animations.
- Smaller image assets.
- No unnecessary real-time WebGL rendering.

Do not rely on a single hardware property to determine device quality. Use actual rendering capability checks and safe fallbacks.

Do not make high-performance mode the only way the website looks correct.

